---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Aside from '../../../components/Aside.astro';
import {funktorSidebar} from '../../../data/funktor-sidebar';
import {Code} from 'astro:components';
---

<DocsLayout title="SaaS" sidebar={funktorSidebar}
            description="Organisations, memberships and a structural cross-tenant isolation boundary">

    <h1>SaaS</h1>

    <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 !mt-2 !mb-8">
        Organisations, memberships, and a boot-enforced boundary that stops one tenant reading another's data.
    </p>

    <h2>Wiring</h2>

    <Code code={`funktor(
    config = config,
    auth = { useKarango() },
    saas = { useKarango() },   // or useMonko()
)`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>This registers the organisation and membership repositories, the <code>orgs</code> API, and &mdash;
        importantly &mdash; the isolation boot check and request guard described below.</p>

    <h2>Storage</h2>

    <p>Two storages, both backend-agnostic (Karango or Monko):</p>

    <Code code={`orgs.findBySlug("acme")
orgs.ensureBySlug(slug = "acme", name = "Acme Inc")
orgs.create(organisation)
orgs.save(organisation)

orgMembers.sessionMembershipsOf(UserId(user._id))   // -> Set<OrgMembership>`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>An organisation carries a <code>slug</code>, a <code>name</code> and an
        <code>OrgStatus</code>. Only <code>Active</code> organisations should be sign-in-able &mdash; see
        <a href="/ultra/funktor/auth">Auth</a> for how a realm turns memberships into a session.</p>

    <h2>Slugs are subdomain-safe</h2>

    <p>Slugs end up in hostnames, so <code>Slugs</code> validates them for that: 2&ndash;63 characters,
        and <code>normalize()</code> trims and lower-cases. <code>validationError(slug)</code> returns a
        human-readable reason rather than a bare boolean, so an API can say what was wrong.</p>

    <h2>Org isolation &mdash; the part that matters</h2>

    <p>Two interfaces turn tenant isolation from a convention into something the framework enforces.</p>

    <Code code={`// 1. Mark the entity as owned by an organisation
data class Invoice(
    override val org: Ref<Organisation>,
    val total: Money,
) : OrgAware

// 2. The route's params must carry the addressed organisation
data class InvoiceParams(
    override val org: Stored<Organisation>,
    val invoice: Stored<Invoice>,
) : OrgAwareParam`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>Marking an entity <code>OrgAware</code> is the developer's opt-in. Once marked:</p>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>At boot</strong>, any route resolving that entity is forced to be
            <code>OrgAwareParam</code> &mdash; a route that loads an org-owned entity without knowing which
            organisation the request addresses fails app start, not review.
        </li>
        <li><strong>At request time</strong>, the guard compares each loaded entity's <code>org</code>
            reference against the request's organisation. A mismatch is refused.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <Aside>The boot check is the interesting half. A runtime guard only protects the routes someone
        remembered to route through it; a boot check protects the ones they forgot, which are the ones
        that leak.
    </Aside>

    <h2>The one contract to get right</h2>

    <p><code>OrgAware.org</code> must carry the organisation's <strong>canonical <code>_id</code></strong>
        (collection-qualified, e.g. <code>organisation/acme</code>), because the guard compares on
        <code>_id</code>. A persisted <code>Ref&lt;Organisation&gt;</code> satisfies this automatically. If
        you construct one by hand, use <code>_id</code> &mdash; never a bare <code>_key</code>.</p>

    <p>Getting it wrong fails <em>closed</em>: every same-org request 404s. That makes it an availability
        landmine rather than a data leak, which is the right direction to be wrong in &mdash; but it is a
        confusing outage, so get it right.</p>

    <Aside>Enforcement lives in the saas module, not in the marker interfaces. An app that uses
        <code>OrgAware</code> without installing <code>funktor(saas = &#123; … &#125;)</code> gets the interfaces and
        none of the checks.
    </Aside>

    <div class="not-prose mt-8">
        <a href="/ultra/funktor/auth"
           class="inline-flex items-center px-6 py-3 bg-brand-600 text-white font-semibold rounded-lg hover:bg-brand-500 transition-colors">
            Auth &amp; realms &rarr;
        </a>
    </div>

</DocsLayout>
